DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The system calls aalllloocc__hhuuggeeppaaggeess() and ffrreeee__hhuuggeeppaaggeess() were introduced
       in Linux 2.5.36 and removed again in Linux 2.5.54.  They  existed  only
       on  i386  and  ia64  (when  built  with CCOONNFFIIGG__HHUUGGEETTLLBB__PPAAGGEE).  In Linux
       2.4.20, the syscall numbers exist, but the calls fail  with  the  error
       EENNOOSSYYSS.

       On  i386  the  memory  management  hardware  knows about ordinary pages
       (4 KiB) and huge pages (2 or 4 MiB).  Similarly ia64 knows  about  huge
       pages  of  several  sizes.   These system calls serve to map huge pages
       into the process’s memory or to free them again.  Huge pages are locked
       into memory, and are not swapped.

       The _k_e_y argument is an identifier.  When zero the  pages  are  private,
       and not inherited by children.  When positive the pages are shared with
       other   applications  using  the  same  _k_e_y,  and  inherited  by  child
       processes.

       The _a_d_d_r argument of ffrreeee__hhuuggeeppaaggeess() tells which page is being  freed:
       it was the return value of a call to aalllloocc__hhuuggeeppaaggeess().  (The memory is
       first  actually freed when all users have released it.)  The _a_d_d_r argu‐
       ment of aalllloocc__hhuuggeeppaaggeess() is a hint, that the kernel  may  or  may  not
       follow.  Addresses must be properly aligned.

       The  _s_i_z_e  argument  is the size of the required segment.  It must be a
       multiple of the huge page size.

       The _p_r_o_t argument specifies the memory protection of the  segment.   It
       is one of PPRROOTT__RREEAADD, PPRROOTT__WWRRIITTEE, PPRROOTT__EEXXEECC.

       The _f_l_a_g argument is ignored, unless _k_e_y is positive.  In that case, if
       _f_l_a_g  is  IIPPCC__CCRREEAATT,  then a new huge page segment is created when none
       with the given key existed.  If this flag is not set,  then  EENNOOEENNTT  is
       returned when no segment with the given key exists.

